</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Generative post-training has firmly established itself as a standard paradigm. While the community has achieved remarkable progress in architectural efficiency (<em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>) and sampling acceleration (<em id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text"></span>, ODE solvers), the principles governing data composition remain largely empirical. Unlike mathematically well-defined optimization mechanisms, current practices still lack systematic theoretical guidance on how data mixtures dictate convergence, often treating datasets as homogeneous signals. This “data-oblivious” approach neglects the complex, often conflicting gradients arising from multi-granular features, creating a hidden bottleneck for efficient adaptation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Key Observation: The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching.</span>
We revisit the mathematical foundations of Flow Matching (FM). We demonstrate that under standard assumptions, for architectures adhering to the FM framework, optimizing the standard Mean Squared Error (MSE) objective is mathematically equivalent to optimizing a latent Quadratic Form, which in turn corresponds to shaping a dynamically evolving Neural Tangent Kernel (NTK). This geometric perspective suggests that the learning dynamics are governed by a Data Interaction Matrix, whose diagonal terms represent the independent learning of specific data samples, while off-diagonal terms encode the residual correlation—constructive or destructive—between heterogeneous data features.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Methodology.</span>
Consequently, fine-tuning is not merely fitting a distribution but seeking a geometric equilibrium within this interaction field. To operationalize this geometric insight within Text-to-Image (T2I) synthesis, we propose Semantic Granularity Alignment (SGA). By engineering targeted interventions in the output-vector residual space, SGA aligns the data structure with the underlying optimization geometry, thereby accelerating convergence while improving generative integrity. Concretely, SGA comprises two mechanisms: (i) <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Tuple-wise Optimization</em>, which constructs multi-granularity semantic tuples so that cross-scale features co-occur within each update step, dampening gradient oscillation; and (ii) <em id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Scale-Adaptive Modulation</em>, which decouples and re-aligns structural and textural learning across distinct denoising frequency bands. Empirically, this alignment improves the efficiency-quality trade-off without significant additional overhead.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conditional Flow Matching.</span>
Diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite> and their continuous-time generalization through Flow Matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> and Rectified Flow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> have established a unified framework for generative modeling, scaled effectively by Rectified Flow Transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> and extended to discrete state-spaces <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> and variational formulations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. Conditional Flow Matching (CFM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite> further generalizes these into a tractable regression objective. Latent Diffusion Models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite> project generation into a compressed latent space, while Classifier-Free Guidance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> has become the de facto conditional sampling strategy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning.</span>
Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> and its variant DoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> enable competitive fine-tuning by constraining weight updates to a low-rank subspace, providing the architectural backbone upon which data-level strategies can be built. LyCORIS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite> further extends this family with alternative decomposition strategies for T2I customization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data-Centric Generative Adaptation.</span>
Engineering solutions such as Aspect Ratio Bucketing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, Zero Terminal SNR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, and community-driven data toolchains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> have become standard in Generative Domain Adaptation (GDA). Subject-driven methods such as DreamBooth <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite> and Textual Inversion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> pioneered few-shot personalization, while ZipLoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> and subsequent efforts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite> advanced few-shot stylized generation. Yet these methods operate at the preprocessing or embedding level; a unified framework connecting data structure design with the optimization objective is less frequently addressed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Theoretical Formulation: The Geometry of Interaction</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We analyze the optimization objective of generative fine-tuning through a geometric lens. We demonstrate that under the Flow Matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> framework, the minimization of the standard Mean Squared Error (MSE) loss can be reformulated as the optimization of a Quadratic Interaction Matrix, which governs the interference between heterogeneous data manifolds.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">The General Objective: Flow Matching (FM).</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a probability path <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> transforming a noise distribution <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to a data distribution <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>. The objective of Flow Matching is to regress a time-dependent vector field <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}(x;\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}(x;\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> that approximates the ground-truth marginal vector field <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{FM}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{t,p_{t}(x)}\left[\|v_{t}(x;\theta)-u_{t}(x)\|^{2}\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{FM}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{t,p_{t}(x)}\left[\|v_{t}(x;\theta)-u_{t}(x)\|^{2}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span> (Linear Superposition of Vector Fields).
We model the data distribution empirically as a mixture of Dirac measures over <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> latent sub-manifolds indexed by <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. Under the CFM framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>, the marginal probability density <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> decomposes linearly over conditionals, and with the standard Gaussian conditional path assumption, the optimal marginal vector field <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed as a probability-weighted superposition of the sub-fields <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{\xi}_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{\xi}_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x)=\sum_{\xi=1}^{N}\alpha_{\xi}(x)\cdot u^{\xi}_{t}(x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x)=\sum_{\xi=1}^{N}\alpha_{\xi}(x)\cdot u^{\xi}_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\xi}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\xi}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the local density (or weight) of the <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>-th sub-manifold.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2</span> (The Quadratic Form of Optimization).
Substituting <a href="#S3.E2" title="In The General Objective: Flow Matching (FM). ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Formulation: The Geometry of Interaction ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> into <a href="#S3.E1" title="In The General Objective: Flow Matching (FM). ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Formulation: The Geometry of Interaction ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and applying the Partition of Unity (<math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\xi}\alpha_{\xi}(x)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\xi}\alpha_{\xi}(x)=1</annotation></semantics></math>), we define the per-granularity residual <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\xi}(x,t)\triangleq v_{\theta}(x,t)-u_{t}^{\xi}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≜</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\xi}(x,t)\triangleq v_{\theta}(x,t)-u_{t}^{\xi}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and expand the squared norm to obtain a Quadratic Form (see Suppl. B for derivation):</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{FM}^{local}(x)=\boldsymbol{\alpha}^{\top}\mathbf{\Omega}\,\boldsymbol{\alpha}=\underbrace{\textstyle\sum_{\xi}\alpha_{\xi}^{2}\|\Delta_{\xi}\|^{2}}_{\text{Independent Learning}}+\underbrace{\textstyle\sum_{\xi\neq\eta}\alpha_{\xi}\alpha_{\eta}\langle\Delta_{\xi},\Delta_{\eta}\rangle}_{\text{Residual Correlation}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝜶</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Independent Learning</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Residual Correlation</mtext></munder></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{FM}^{local}(x)=\boldsymbol{\alpha}^{\top}\mathbf{\Omega}\,\boldsymbol{\alpha}=\underbrace{\textstyle\sum_{\xi}\alpha_{\xi}^{2}\|\Delta_{\xi}\|^{2}}_{\text{Independent Learning}}+\underbrace{\textstyle\sum_{\xi\neq\eta}\alpha_{\xi}\alpha_{\eta}\langle\Delta_{\xi},\Delta_{\eta}\rangle}_{\text{Residual Correlation}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝛀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math> is the symmetric <em id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Data Interference Matrix</em> with entries <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{\xi\eta}=\langle\Delta_{\xi},\Delta_{\eta}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\xi\eta}=\langle\Delta_{\xi},\Delta_{\eta}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3</span> (NTK Emergence in Gradient Space).
Let <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{\theta}(x,t)=\partial v_{\theta}/\partial\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{\theta}(x,t)=\partial v_{\theta}/\partial\theta</annotation></semantics></math> denote the network Jacobian. By the chain rule, the total parameter gradient decomposes as <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\text{total}}=J_{\theta}^{\top}e(x,t)=\sum_{\xi}g_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></msub><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\text{total}}=J_{\theta}^{\top}e(x,t)=\sum_{\xi}g_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(x,t)\triangleq v_{\theta}(x,t)-u_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≜</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(x,t)\triangleq v_{\theta}(x,t)-u_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> denoting the prediction residual, where <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\xi}\triangleq\alpha_{\xi}\,J_{\theta}^{\top}\Delta_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo>≜</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\xi}\triangleq\alpha_{\xi}\,J_{\theta}^{\top}\Delta_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Suppl. E). The pairwise gradient interaction:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle g_{\xi},g_{\eta}\rangle=\alpha_{\xi}\alpha_{\eta}\,\Delta_{\xi}^{\top}\underbrace{J_{\theta}J_{\theta}^{\top}}_{\Theta_{\theta}(x,t)}\Delta_{\eta}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle g_{\xi},g_{\eta}\rangle=\alpha_{\xi}\alpha_{\eta}\,\Delta_{\xi}^{\top}\underbrace{J_{\theta}J_{\theta}^{\top}}_{\Theta_{\theta}(x,t)}\Delta_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">reveals the Neural Tangent Kernel (NTK) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{\theta}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{\theta}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math>: the output-space geometry of <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math> directly governs the parameter-space gradient dynamics through the NTK.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.10785v1/figure_interaction_matrix.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/234;" width="476" height="234"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Structure of the Data Interference Matrix <math id="S3.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math>. Diagonal entries (blue) represent independent learning within each sub-manifold. Off-diagonal entries encode cross-scale interactions: constructive (synergy, green) or destructive (conflict, red).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1</span> (Optimization Equivalence).
Since the marginal vector field <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is generally intractable, Conditional Flow Matching (CFM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite> is employed as a proxy, typically instantiated as a Mean Squared Error (MSE) loss. Given that <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}_{FM}=\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}_{CFM}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}_{FM}=\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}_{CFM}</annotation></semantics></math>, we establish the following relationship:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S3.Ex1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">Optimizing MSE</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\iff\text{Optimizing CFM}\iff\text{Optimizing FM}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><mtext>Optimizing CFM</mtext><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><mtext>Optimizing FM</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\iff\text{Optimizing CFM}\iff\text{Optimizing FM}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\iff\min_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\mathbb{E}_{x,t}\!\left[\boldsymbol{\alpha}^{\top}\mathbf{\Omega}(\theta)\,\boldsymbol{\alpha}\right]\iff\text{Shaping }\Theta_{\theta}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝜶</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><mrow><mtext>Shaping </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\iff\min_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\mathbb{E}_{x,t}\!\left[\boldsymbol{\alpha}^{\top}\mathbf{\Omega}(\theta)\,\boldsymbol{\alpha}\right]\iff\text{Shaping }\Theta_{\theta}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This derivation highlights the intrinsic geometry of fine-tuning: the MSE objective is, in effect, training a vector residual field governed by a dynamically evolving NTK. However, the intrinsic complexity of the NTK renders direct analytical intervention intractable. We therefore adopt an engineering approach: rather than manipulating the kernel itself, we intervene in the vector residual field by restructuring the data that shapes it. Specifically, we first apply Hierarchical Semantic Decomposition (H-SD) to partition the original dataset into three semantically distinct sub-manifolds—Macro (global structure), Meso (mid-level layout), and Micro (fine-grained texture)—so that the training set is reorganized as <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=\bigcup_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}Y_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=\bigcup_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}Y_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}=\{\text{Macro},\text{Meso},\text{Micro}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext>Macro</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>Meso</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>Micro</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}=\{\text{Macro},\text{Meso},\text{Micro}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. By the linearity of the conditional vector field (Eq. <a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ The General Objective: Flow Matching (FM). ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Formulation: The Geometry of Interaction ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), each entry of the resulting matrix captures the vector-field residual associated with a particular sub-manifold rather than a mere granularity label; we therefore term the instantiated <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math> the <em id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Semantic Interference Matrix</em>. The diagonal entries <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{\xi\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\xi\xi}</annotation></semantics></math> capture the self-alignment error within each granularity, while the off-diagonal entries <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{\xi\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\xi\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> quantify cross-scale semantic interference. When opposing residual directions arise in output space (<em id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span>, <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\Delta_{\text{Macro}},\Delta_{\text{Micro}}\rangle&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mtext>Macro</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mtext>Micro</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\Delta_{\text{Macro}},\Delta_{\text{Micro}}\rangle&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>), this divergence propagates through the network Jacobian into parameter space, inducing gradient conflict and learning oscillation across update steps. Standard fine-tuning handles this off-diagonal interference only implicitly through stochastic sampling; our SGA framework instead provides an explicit, structured mechanism to regulate it.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>The Fine-Tuning Dilemma</h3>

<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.10785v1/figure_pipeline_sketch.svg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:371/173;" width="371" height="173"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The optimization landscape <math id="S3.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{FM}=\boldsymbol{\alpha}^{\top}\mathbf{\Omega}\,\boldsymbol{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝜶</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{FM}=\boldsymbol{\alpha}^{\top}\mathbf{\Omega}\,\boldsymbol{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> of CFM fine-tuning. <span id="S3.F3.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Path 1</span> (red): Underfitting Region. <span id="S3.F3.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Path 3</span> (blue): OOD Region. Only <span id="S3.F3.7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Path 2</span> (green) navigates the narrow Goal Region.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.10785v1/figure_failure_cases_2.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/173;" width="476" height="173" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S3.F4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Failure analysis of standard fine-tuning. All outputs exhibit dominant prior characteristics regardless of target domain—a <em id="S3.F4.5.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">prior capture</em> phenomenon that traps the model in the Underfitting Region of <a href="#S3.F3" title="In 3.2 The Fine-Tuning Dilemma ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in <a href="#S3.F3" title="In 3.2 The Fine-Tuning Dilemma ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, drawing on well-established perspectives from optimization geometry and generalization theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, the optimization landscape exhibits three regimes: an <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Underfitting Region</em> where the pre-trained prior suppresses downstream updates, an <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">OOD Region</em> where aggressive training causes catastrophic forgetting, and a narrow <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Sweet Spot</em> between them. Standard fine-tuning frequently falls into the underfitting regime (<a href="#S3.F4" title="In 3.2 The Fine-Tuning Dilemma ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), while aggressive hyperparameter tuning often overshoots into the OOD region. This directly motivates our framework: H-SD (<a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Construction of the Hierarchical Semantic Dataset ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>) amplifies the downstream signal to escape underfitting, while Tuple-wise Optimization (<a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Tuple-wise Optimization Protocol ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>) and Scale-Adaptive Modulation (<a href="#S3.SS5" title="3.5 Scale-Adaptive Modulation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>) prevent OOD drift.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Construction of the Hierarchical Semantic Dataset</h3>

<figure id="S3.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.10785v1/figure_hsd_pipeline.png" id="S3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/364;" width="476" height="364" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F5.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S3.F5.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Overview of the Hierarchical Semantic Decomposition (H-SD) pipeline. Raw images are parsed by a general-purpose detector (<em id="S3.F5.6.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S3.F5.6.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, multimodal models, YOLO, or Grounding DINO) into three granularities (<math id="S3.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\text{Macro}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mtext>Macro</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\text{Macro}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.F5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\text{Meso}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mtext>Meso</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\text{Meso}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.F5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\text{Micro}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mtext>Micro</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\text{Micro}}</annotation></semantics></math>), filtered via IoU-based redundancy elimination, and post-processed into aspect-ratio-preserving resolution buckets.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To instantiate the theoretical sub-manifolds from <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Theoretical Formulation: The Geometry of Interaction ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, we implement the H-SD pipeline (<a href="#S3.F5" title="In 3.3 Construction of the Hierarchical Semantic Dataset ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). A general-purpose object detector (<em id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, multimodal models, YOLO, or Grounding DINO) first parses each image into three semantic tiers—Subject (Macro), Sub-structures (Meso), and Details (Micro)—while preserving Part-Whole traceability. We then apply IoU-based filtering across hierarchies to discard spatially redundant slices, ensuring that each sub-manifold contributes distinct information to <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math>. The retained slices are further refined through a post-processing pipeline that includes aspect-ratio-preserving downsampling and resolution bucketing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, amplifying local features that are typically diluted in global objectives.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Tuple-wise Optimization Protocol</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although the standard MSE objective implicitly optimizes the full quadratic form <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\alpha}^{\top}\mathbf{\Omega}\,\boldsymbol{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝜶</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\alpha}^{\top}\mathbf{\Omega}\,\boldsymbol{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, when semantic slices (<em id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, Macro and Micro) are sampled into separate batches, each gradient step is usually dominated by a single scale’s contribution. Alternating between such scale-biased updates produces oscillating gradient directions, a phenomenon well-documented in multi-task optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>. To address this, we construct <em id="S3.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">semantic tuples</em> that enforce the co-occurrence of hierarchically related slices within the same batch. For a set of <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> semantic scales indexed by <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, the joint loss is:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{Tuple}(\theta)=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\lambda_{k}\cdot\mathcal{L}_{CFM}(\theta;\,\mathcal{B}_{k})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{Tuple}(\theta)=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\lambda_{k}\cdot\mathcal{L}_{CFM}(\theta;\,\mathcal{B}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is the subset of slices at granularity <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> within the tuple and <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is a balancing coefficient (set to <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0</annotation></semantics></math>). By co-sampling all scales in a single optimization step, we attempt to engineer an intervention on the output of the vector residual field, encouraging the per-step gradient to balance contributions from both the diagonal (self-alignment) and off-diagonal (cross-scale residual correlation) terms of <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Scale-Adaptive Modulation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Tuple-wise protocol enforces spatial consistency but does not address the spectral discrepancy between granularities: Macro slices are dominated by low-frequency geometry, while Micro slices encode high-frequency texture. A uniform optimization schedule conflates these distinct signal bands, introducing cross-frequency noise. Prior work has shown that the noise-level sampling strategy significantly affects both convergence speed and final generation quality in diffusion and flow-based models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. We resolve this via two architecture-specific mechanisms (see Suppl. D for derivations):</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS5.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Frequency-Time Alignment (For DiT Architectures).</h4>

<div id="S3.SS5.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In MM-DiT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> models (<em id="S3.SS5.SSSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S3.SS5.SSSx1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, FLUX.1), where time-step sampling follows a Logit-Normal distribution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, global structure emerges at high noise levels (<math id="S3.SS5.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\to 1</annotation></semantics></math>) and fine details at low levels (<math id="S3.SS5.SSSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>). We condition the time-step sampling <math id="S3.SS5.SSSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(t)</annotation></semantics></math> on scale <math id="S3.SS5.SSSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, shifting probability mass toward <math id="S3.SS5.SSSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\to 1</annotation></semantics></math> for Macro (structural focus) and toward <math id="S3.SS5.SSSx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> for Micro (textural refinement).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">SNR-Aware Reweighting (For U-Net Architectures).</h4>

<div id="S3.SS5.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For U-Net models (<em id="S3.SS5.SSSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S3.SS5.SSSx2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, SDXL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>), we modulate the loss weight <math id="S3.SS5.SSSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(t,S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(t,S)</annotation></semantics></math> by granularity, building on insights from Zero Terminal SNR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>: increasing the weight for Micro slices in high-SNR regimes to sustain gradient supervision on fine details, and reducing it for Macro slices to prevent overfitting to compression artifacts.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.10785v1/figure_loss_landscape.svg" id="S3.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/383;" width="476" height="383"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F6.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S3.F6.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Optimization trajectories on the H-SD-augmented loss landscape. <span id="S3.F6.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">H-SD + Standard Opt.</span> (orange): after signal amplification via H-SD, vanilla CFM training still suffers from scale-biased gradient updates, causing persistent oscillation and drift toward the OOD boundary. <span id="S3.F6.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SGA</span> (blue): by adding Tuple-wise co-sampling and Scale-Adaptive Modulation, SGA stabilizes the descent and converges to the Goal Region. The residual correlation sign <math id="S3.F6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\Delta_{\xi},\Delta_{\eta}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em" stretchy="true">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em" stretchy="true">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\Delta_{\xi},\Delta_{\eta}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> determines whether cross-scale interactions are constructive (<math id="S3.F6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, stable) or destructive (<math id="S3.F6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, oscillation).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets &amp; Scope.</span> 
We curated diverse GDA datasets with 100 to several hundred images per domain. For the MM-DiT architecture (FLUX), we evaluate on 6 datasets; for the U-Net architecture (Animagine XL 3.1), we select 3 datasets to validate cross-architecture generalizability (full experimental setup in Suppl. F).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Architectures &amp; Baselines.</span> 
We evaluate SGA on two frameworks: (1) <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DiT:</span> FLUX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> with DoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> and AdamW <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>; (2) <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">U-Net:</span> Animagine XL 3.1 (SDXL-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>) with LoCon <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite> and Lion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. We compare against standard fine-tuning with Aspect Ratio Bucketing (ARB) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> and horizontal flipping; SGA is orthogonal to ARB and fully compatible with its resolution logic. All hyperparameters are identical between baseline and SGA.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training &amp; Implementation.</span> 
Learning rates follow community-established defaults. Following the critical batch size principle <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>, we set batch sizes according to model scale: FLUX uses 8, Animagine XL 3.1 uses 2. Training budgets are reported as multiples of a reference GPU-time <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> calibrated per dataset. All experiments run on NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell GPUs (96 GB GDDR7).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Qualitative Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present visual comparisons across six GDA domains (<a href="#S4.F7" title="In 4.2 Qualitative Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figs.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 4.2 Qualitative Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>).</p>
</div>
