<section id="Pt0.A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix 0.A </span>Derivation of Granularity-Specific Vector Fields</h2>

<div id="Pt0.A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide the rigorous derivation of Theorem 1 presented in the main text. We prove that under the Conditional Flow Matching (CFM) framework, the optimal marginal vector field <math id="Pt0.A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> decomposes into a probability-weighted superposition of sub-fields generated by distinct data granularities.</p>
</div>
<section id="Pt0.A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">0.A.1 </span>The Marginal Probability as a Gaussian Mixture</h3>

<div id="Pt0.A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by defining the marginal probability density <math id="Pt0.A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="Pt0.A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. By definition, the marginal distribution is the expectation of the conditional distribution over the data distribution:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A1.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A1.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x)=\int p_{t}(x|x_{1})q(x_{1})dx_{1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)=\int p_{t}(x|x_{1})q(x_{1})dx_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the context of fine-tuning, we operate on a discrete dataset <math id="Pt0.A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{x^{(1)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{x^{(1)},</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="Pt0.A1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\dots,x^{(N)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dots,x^{(N)}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The true data distribution <math id="Pt0.A1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(x_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(x_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is approximated by the empirical distribution <math id="Pt0.A1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{q}(x_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}(x_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A1.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A1.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(x_{1})\approx\hat{q}(x_{1})=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{k=1}^{N}\delta(x_{1}-x^{(k)})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(x_{1})\approx\hat{q}(x_{1})=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{k=1}^{N}\delta(x_{1}-x^{(k)})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Substituting this empirical distribution into the integral, and utilizing the linearity of integration to interchange the integral and summation operators, we obtain:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A1.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A1.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{k=1}^{N}\left[\int p_{t}(x|x_{1})\cdot\delta(x_{1}-x^{(k)})dx_{1}\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{k=1}^{N}\left[\int p_{t}(x|x_{1})\cdot\delta(x_{1}-x^{(k)})dx_{1}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Applying the Sifting Property of the Dirac delta function, which states that <math id="Pt0.A1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int f(z)\delta(z-a)dz=f(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int f(z)\delta(z-a)dz=f(a)</annotation></semantics></math>, the integral simplifies to evaluating the conditional density at the specific sample <math id="Pt0.A1.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Crucially, within the CFM framework, the conditional probability path <math id="Pt0.A1.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x|x_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x|x_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="Pt0.A1.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">chosen</em> to be Gaussian by construction—this is a design choice of the flow, not an assumption about the data distribution. Any arbitrary data distribution <math id="Pt0.A1.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(x_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(x_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, regardless of its complexity or modality, is compatible with Gaussian conditional paths. Consequently, the marginal distribution <math id="Pt0.A1.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> takes the form of a Gaussian Mixture Model (GMM):</p>
<table id="Pt0.A1.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A1.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{k=1}^{N}\mathcal{N}(x|\mu_{t}(x^{(k)}),\sigma_{t}(x^{(k)})^{2}I)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{k=1}^{N}\mathcal{N}(x|\mu_{t}(x^{(k)}),\sigma_{t}(x^{(k)})^{2}I)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">0.A.2 </span>The Marginal Vector Field as a Nadaraya-Watson Estimator</h3>

<div id="Pt0.A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now derive the expression for the marginal vector field <math id="Pt0.A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. The target vector field is defined as the ratio of the aggregate momentum density to the probability density:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A1.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A1.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x)=\frac{\int u_{t}(x|x_{1})p_{t}(x|x_{1})q(x_{1})dx_{1}}{p_{t}(x)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x)=\frac{\int u_{t}(x|x_{1})p_{t}(x|x_{1})q(x_{1})dx_{1}}{p_{t}(x)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Applying the same Dirac sifting logic to the numerator, we arrive at the closed-form expression for <math id="Pt0.A1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A1.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A1.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x)=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}u_{t}(x|x^{(i)})\cdot p_{t}(x|x^{(i)})}{\sum_{k=1}^{N}p_{t}(x|x^{(k)})}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x)=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}u_{t}(x|x^{(i)})\cdot p_{t}(x|x^{(i)})}{\sum_{k=1}^{N}p_{t}(x|x^{(k)})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This form is mathematically equivalent to the Nadaraya-Watson kernel regression estimator. It implies that the optimal vector field at any point <math id="Pt0.A1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is a weighted average of the conditional vector fields provided by each sample.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">0.A.3 </span>Decomposition by Data Granularity</h3>

<div id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generality of the partition.</span>
The decomposition derived in the preceding sections holds for <em id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">any</em> partition of the training set into disjoint groups—the only mathematical requirement is that the index sets <math id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{I}_{\xi}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{I}_{\xi}\}</annotation></semantics></math> form a valid partition of <math id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\dots,N\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\dots,N\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Semantic segmentation (as adopted in our H-SD pipeline) is one convenient instantiation, but it is by no means the only one.
Any criterion that assigns each sample to exactly one group yields a valid partition, for example:
<em id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(i)</em> frequency-band clustering in the latent space,
<em id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(ii)</em> resolution or aspect-ratio bucketing,
<em id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(iii)</em> caption-level topic grouping,
or <em id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(iv)</em> even a random split.
The algebraic identities (<a href="#Pt0.A1.E14" title="In 0.A.3 Decomposition by Data Granularity ‣ Appendix 0.A Derivation of Granularity-Specific Vector Fields ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eqs.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> and <a href="#Pt0.A2.E20" title="Equation 20 ‣ 0.B.3 The Data Interference Matrix 𝛀 ‣ Appendix 0.B Proof of the Data Interference Matrix ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) are invariant to the choice of grouping criterion; what changes across different partitions is only the <em id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">practical effectiveness</em> of the resulting sub-fields in reducing cross-scale interference.</p>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, let <math id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> be an arbitrary finite label set (with <math id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{T}|\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{T}|\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>) and let <math id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{I}_{\xi}\}_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{I}_{\xi}\}_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a partition of the dataset indices, <em id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, <math id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}_{\xi}\cap\mathcal{I}_{\eta}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}_{\xi}\cap\mathcal{I}_{\eta}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi\neq\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi\neq\eta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\mathcal{I}_{\xi}=\{1,\dots,N\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\mathcal{I}_{\xi}=\{1,\dots,N\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In our experiments we set <math id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}=\{a,b,c\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}=\{a,b,c\}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to Macro, Meso, and Micro granularities obtained via hierarchical semantic decomposition, but the derivation below is stated for a general <math id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We define the Aggregate Local Density <math id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\xi}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\xi}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and the Group Vector Field <math id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}^{\xi}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}^{\xi}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for each group <math id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi\in\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi\in\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A1.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A1.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\xi}(x)=\sum_{k\in\mathcal{I}_{\xi}}p_{t}(x|x^{(k)}),\quad u_{t}^{\xi}(x)=\frac{1}{Z_{\xi}(x)}\sum_{k\in\mathcal{I}_{\xi}}p_{t}(x|x^{(k)})u_{t}(x|x^{(k)})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\xi}(x)=\sum_{k\in\mathcal{I}_{\xi}}p_{t}(x|x^{(k)}),\quad u_{t}^{\xi}(x)=\frac{1}{Z_{\xi}(x)}\sum_{k\in\mathcal{I}_{\xi}}p_{t}(x|x^{(k)})u_{t}(x|x^{(k)})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Note that since the Gaussian conditional density satisfies <math id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x|x^{(k)})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x|x^{(k)})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, the aggregate density <math id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\xi}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\xi}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly positive for any non-empty partition, rendering the divisions in the equations above and below universally well-defined without singularities.</p>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We further define the global unnormalized density <math id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\text{total}}(x)=\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{T}}Z_{\eta}(x)=\sum_{k=1}^{N}p_{t}(x|x^{(k)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\text{total}}(x)=\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{T}}Z_{\eta}(x)=\sum_{k=1}^{N}p_{t}(x|x^{(k)})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Substituting these back into the Nadaraya-Watson estimator, we obtain the final decomposed form:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A1.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A1.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x)=\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\frac{Z_{\xi}(x)}{Z_{\text{total}}(x)}u_{t}^{\xi}(x)=\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)u_{t}^{\xi}(x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x)=\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\frac{Z_{\xi}(x)}{Z_{\text{total}}(x)}u_{t}^{\xi}(x)=\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)u_{t}^{\xi}(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\xi}(x)=Z_{\xi}(x)/Z_{\text{total}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\xi}(x)=Z_{\xi}(x)/Z_{\text{total}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the local data density (or voting power) of group <math id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math> at position <math id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that by definition, <math id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\xi}\alpha_{\xi}(x)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\xi}\alpha_{\xi}(x)=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark.</span>
<a href="#Pt0.A1.E14" title="In 0.A.3 Decomposition by Data Granularity ‣ Appendix 0.A Derivation of Granularity-Specific Vector Fields ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> is a purely algebraic identity that follows from the partition structure of <math id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{I}_{\xi}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{I}_{\xi}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and the linearity of summation; it does <em id="Pt0.A1.SS3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> depend on how the groups are constructed.
Consequently, the entire downstream analysis—including the Data Interference Matrix (<a href="#Pt0.A2" title="Appendix 0.B Proof of the Data Interference Matrix ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">0.B</span></a>) and the NTK-based gradient dynamics (<a href="#Pt0.A5" title="Appendix 0.E General Framework: Bridging Output-Space Residuals and Parameter-Space Gradient Dynamics ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">0.E</span></a>)—applies to any valid partition of the dataset.
Semantic segmentation is the particular instantiation we adopt in practice because it produces groups with well-separated frequency spectra, but the algebraic apparatus itself is general and applies to any valid partition.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix 0.B </span>Proof of the Data Interference Matrix</h2>

<div id="Pt0.A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we derive Theorem 2, demonstrating that the CFM loss function inherently minimizes a quadratic form governed by a data interference matrix.</p>
</div>
<section id="Pt0.A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">0.B.1 </span>Loss Decomposition via Partition of Unity</h3>

<div id="Pt0.A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Locally, at a specific spacetime point <math id="Pt0.A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math>, the squared error between the model output and the marginal target field is defined as:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A2.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A2.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{Kernel}}(x)=\|v_{t}(x)-u_{t}(x)\|^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>Kernel</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{Kernel}}(x)=\|v_{t}(x)-u_{t}(x)\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Pt0.A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Substituting the decomposed target field <math id="Pt0.A2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x)=\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)u_{t}^{\xi}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x)=\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)u_{t}^{\xi}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, and crucially, utilizing the Partition of Unity property <math id="Pt0.A2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)=1</annotation></semantics></math> to rewrite the model output as <math id="Pt0.A2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}(x)=\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)v_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}(x)=\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)v_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, we can regroup the error terms:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A2.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A2.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{Kernel}}(x)=\left\|\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)\underbrace{(v_{t}(x)-u_{t}^{\xi}(x))}_{\Delta_{\xi}(x)}\right\|^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>Kernel</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{Kernel}}(x)=\left\|\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)\underbrace{(v_{t}(x)-u_{t}^{\xi}(x))}_{\Delta_{\xi}(x)}\right\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Pt0.A2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\xi}(x)=v_{t}(x)-u_{t}^{\xi}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\xi}(x)=v_{t}(x)-u_{t}^{\xi}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the error vector relative to data source <math id="Pt0.A2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">0.B.2 </span>Expansion into Quadratic Form</h3>

<div id="Pt0.A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Expanding the squared norm of the weighted sum <math id="Pt0.A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\xi}\alpha_{\xi}\Delta_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\xi}\alpha_{\xi}\Delta_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math>, we apply the identity <math id="Pt0.A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\sum_{i}v_{i}\|^{2}=\sum_{i}\sum_{j}\langle v_{i},v_{j}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\sum_{i}v_{i}\|^{2}=\sum_{i}\sum_{j}\langle v_{i},v_{j}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A2.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A2.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{Kernel}}(x)=\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)\alpha_{\eta}(x)\langle\Delta_{\xi}(x),\Delta_{\eta}(x)\rangle" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>Kernel</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{Kernel}}(x)=\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)\alpha_{\eta}(x)\langle\Delta_{\xi}(x),\Delta_{\eta}(x)\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This expansion reveals two distinct interactions:</p>
<ul id="Pt0.A2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Pt0.A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Pt0.A2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Pt0.A2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Self-Alignment Terms</span> (Diagonal, <math id="Pt0.A2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi=\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi=\eta</annotation></semantics></math>): <math id="Pt0.A2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\xi}^{2}\|\Delta_{\xi}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\xi}^{2}\|\Delta_{\xi}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, representing the independent fitting error for each data source.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Pt0.A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Pt0.A2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Pt0.A2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cross-Interference Terms</span> (Off-Diagonal, <math id="Pt0.A2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi\neq\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi\neq\eta</annotation></semantics></math>): <math id="Pt0.A2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\xi}\alpha_{\eta}\langle\Delta_{\xi},\Delta_{\eta}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\xi}\alpha_{\eta}\langle\Delta_{\xi},\Delta_{\eta}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, representing the residual correlation between data sources.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">0.B.3 </span>The Data Interference Matrix <math id="Pt0.A2.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To compactly represent this structure, we introduce the following definitions. Let <math id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> be the label set of data granularities with <math id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{T}|=N_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{T}|=N_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 1: Entry-wise definition.</span> 
We define the symmetric positive semi-definite (PSD) <em id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Data Interference Matrix</em> <math id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{g}\times N_{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝛀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{g}\times N_{g}}</annotation></semantics></math> with entries:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A2.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A2.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{\xi\eta}(x)=\langle\Delta_{\xi}(x),\Delta_{\eta}(x)\rangle,\quad\xi,\eta\in\mathcal{T}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\xi\eta}(x)=\langle\Delta_{\xi}(x),\Delta_{\eta}(x)\rangle,\quad\xi,\eta\in\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Mathematically, <math id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝛀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the Gram matrix of the residual vectors <math id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\Delta_{\xi}(x)\}_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\Delta_{\xi}(x)\}_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which guarantees positive semi-definiteness by construction. This ensures <math id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\alpha}^{\top}\mathbf{\Omega}\,\boldsymbol{\alpha}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝜶</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\alpha}^{\top}\mathbf{\Omega}\,\boldsymbol{\alpha}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜶</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, consistent with the physical interpretation that the squared Euclidean distance is non-negative.</p>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 2: Weighting vector.</span> 
We define the density weighting vector <math id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\alpha}(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\alpha}(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{g}}</annotation></semantics></math> whose <math id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>-th component is <math id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\xi}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\xi}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, <em id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span>,</p>
<table id="Pt0.A2.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A2.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\alpha}(x)=\big[\alpha_{\xi_{1}}(x),\,\alpha_{\xi_{2}}(x),\,\dots,\,\alpha_{\xi_{N_{g}}}(x)\big]^{\top},\quad\text{with }\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)=1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>ξ</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>with </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\alpha}(x)=\big[\alpha_{\xi_{1}}(x),\,\alpha_{\xi_{2}}(x),\,\dots,\,\alpha_{\xi_{N_{g}}}(x)\big]^{\top},\quad\text{with }\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)=1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 3: Quadratic form.</span> 
Substituting these definitions, the local loss function is strictly reformulated as a quadratic form:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A2.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A2.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{Kernel}}(x)=\boldsymbol{\alpha}(x)^{\top}\mathbf{\Omega}(x)\,\boldsymbol{\alpha}(x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>Kernel</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝜶</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝜶</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{Kernel}}(x)=\boldsymbol{\alpha}(x)^{\top}\mathbf{\Omega}(x)\,\boldsymbol{\alpha}(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This formulation holds for any finite partition <math id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> of arbitrary cardinality <math id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{g}\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{g}\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. The optimization landscape is mathematically governed by <math id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝛀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>: diagonal entries <math id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{\xi\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\xi\xi}</annotation></semantics></math> capture the self-alignment error within each granularity, while off-diagonal entries <math id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{\xi\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\xi\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi\neq\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi\neq\eta</annotation></semantics></math>) quantify the constructive or destructive interference between data sources. In our experiments, we instantiate <math id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}=\{\text{Macro},\text{Meso},\text{Micro}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext>Macro</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>Meso</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>Micro</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}=\{\text{Macro},\text{Meso},\text{Micro}\}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Pt0.A2.SS3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{g}=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{g}=3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix 0.C </span>Derivation of the General Flow Matching Objective</h2>

<div id="Pt0.A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we connect the data-source interference analysis from <a href="#Pt0.A2" title="Appendix 0.B Proof of the Data Interference Matrix ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">0.B</span></a> back to the general Flow Matching (FM) framework. We derive the tractable training objective by transitioning from the marginal vector field to the conditional vector field, utilizing a Gaussian reparameterization path.</p>
</div>
<section id="Pt0.A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">0.C.1 </span>Equivalence to the Global FM Objective</h3>

<div id="Pt0.A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall from Theorem 2 (<a href="#Pt0.A2" title="Appendix 0.B Proof of the Data Interference Matrix ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">0.B</span></a>) that the local loss at any spacetime point <math id="Pt0.A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> is governed by the quadratic form of the data density weights <math id="Pt0.A3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\alpha}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\alpha}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and the interference matrix <math id="Pt0.A3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝛀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A3.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A3.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{local}}(x)=\boldsymbol{\alpha}(x)^{\top}\mathbf{\Omega}(x)\boldsymbol{\alpha}(x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>local</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝜶</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝜶</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{local}}(x)=\boldsymbol{\alpha}(x)^{\top}\mathbf{\Omega}(x)\boldsymbol{\alpha}(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By reversing the decomposition in <a href="#Pt0.A2" title="Appendix 0.B Proof of the Data Interference Matrix ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">0.B</span></a>, this quadratic form is strictly mathematically equivalent to the squared Euclidean distance between the model output <math id="Pt0.A3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\theta}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\theta}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> and the true marginal vector field <math id="Pt0.A3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A3.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A3.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\alpha}(x)^{\top}\mathbf{\Omega}(x)\boldsymbol{\alpha}(x)\equiv\|v_{\theta}(x,t)-u_{t}(x)\|^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝜶</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝜶</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\alpha}(x)^{\top}\mathbf{\Omega}(x)\boldsymbol{\alpha}(x)\equiv\|v_{\theta}(x,t)-u_{t}(x)\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Consequently, the global Flow Matching objective is defined as the expectation of this local error over time <math id="Pt0.A3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and the marginal data distribution <math id="Pt0.A3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A3.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A3.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{FM}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{t\sim\pi(t)}\left[\mathbb{E}_{x\sim p_{t}(x)}\left[\|v_{\theta}(x,t)-u_{t}(x)\|^{2}\right]\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{FM}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{t\sim\pi(t)}\left[\mathbb{E}_{x\sim p_{t}(x)}\left[\|v_{\theta}(x,t)-u_{t}(x)\|^{2}\right]\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Pt0.A3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Pt0.A3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark:</span> The marginal vector field <math id="Pt0.A3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#Pt0.A3.E23" title="In 0.C.1 Equivalence to the Global FM Objective ‣ Appendix 0.C Derivation of the General Flow Matching Objective ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a> is computationally intractable, as it requires integrating over the entire data distribution <math id="Pt0.A3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(x_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(x_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> to evaluate the marginal density <math id="Pt0.A3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and its derivative.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">0.C.2 </span>Tractability via Conditional Flow Matching</h3>

<div id="Pt0.A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To eliminate the intractable <math id="Pt0.A3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, we invoke the core theorem of Conditional Flow Matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>, which states that fitting the marginal vector field is equivalent to fitting the conditional vector fields <math id="Pt0.A3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x|x_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x|x_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> under the expectation over data.</p>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By swapping the order of integration (demarginalization), we obtain the Conditional Flow Matching (CFM) objective:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A3.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A3.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{CFM}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{t\sim\pi(t)}\left[\mathbb{E}_{x_{1}\sim q(x_{1})}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim p_{t}(x|x_{1})}\left[\|v_{\theta}(x,t)-u_{t}(x|x_{1})\|^{2}\right]\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{CFM}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{t\sim\pi(t)}\left[\mathbb{E}_{x_{1}\sim q(x_{1})}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim p_{t}(x|x_{1})}\left[\|v_{\theta}(x,t)-u_{t}(x|x_{1})\|^{2}\right]\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Here, the target field shifts from the aggregate marginal field <math id="Pt0.A3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> to the tractable single-sample conditional field <math id="Pt0.A3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x|x_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x|x_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">0.C.3 </span>Reparameterization for Gaussian Paths</h3>

<div id="Pt0.A3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the inner expectation efficiently, we assume the conditional probability path <math id="Pt0.A3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x|x_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x|x_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> follows a general Gaussian distribution:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A3.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A3.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x|x_{1})=\mathcal{N}(x\,|\,\mu_{t}(x_{1}),\sigma_{t}(x_{1})^{2}\mathbf{I})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x|x_{1})=\mathcal{N}(x\,|\,\mu_{t}(x_{1}),\sigma_{t}(x_{1})^{2}\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We apply the reparameterization trick using standard normal noise <math id="Pt0.A3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Pt0.A3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Pt0.A3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sample Location</span> (<math id="Pt0.A3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>):</p>
<table id="Pt0.A3.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A3.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=\Psi_{t}(x_{1})=\mu_{t}(x_{1})+\sigma_{t}(x_{1})\cdot\epsilon" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=\Psi_{t}(x_{1})=\mu_{t}(x_{1})+\sigma_{t}(x_{1})\cdot\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Pt0.A3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Pt0.A3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Compute Target Flow</span> (<math id="Pt0.A3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>):
The target vector field is defined as the time derivative of the flow map <math id="Pt0.A3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{t}(x_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{t}(x_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A3.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A3.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x|x_{1})=\frac{d}{dt}\Psi_{t}(x_{1})=\dot{\mu}_{t}(x_{1})+\dot{\sigma}_{t}(x_{1})\cdot\epsilon" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x|x_{1})=\frac{d}{dt}\Psi_{t}(x_{1})=\dot{\mu}_{t}(x_{1})+\dot{\sigma}_{t}(x_{1})\cdot\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Pt0.A3.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Pt0.A3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\mu}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\mu}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Pt0.A3.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\sigma}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\sigma}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the partial derivatives with respect to time <math id="Pt0.A3.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">0.C.4 </span>The Empirical Batch Estimator</h3>

<div id="Pt0.A3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we discretize the expectations into a batch-computable loss function. Let <math id="Pt0.A3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}=\{x_{1}^{(1)},\dots,x_{1}^{(B)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}=\{x_{1}^{(1)},\dots,x_{1}^{(B)}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be a batch of data samples, <math id="Pt0.A3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\sim\pi(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\sim\pi(t)</annotation></semantics></math> be sampled times, and <math id="Pt0.A3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math> be sampled noise.</p>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The general CFM loss function is given by:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A3.E28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A3.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{General}}(\theta)\!=\!\frac{1}{B}\!\sum_{i=1}^{B}\left\|v_{\theta}\!\Big(\underbrace{\mu_{t_{i}}\!(x_{1}^{(i)})\!+\!\sigma_{t_{i}}\!(x_{1}^{(i)})\!\cdot\!\epsilon_{i}}_{\text{Input }x_{t_{i}}},\,t_{i}\Big)\!-\!\underbrace{\Big(\dot{\mu}_{t_{i}}\!(x_{1}^{(i)})\!+\!\dot{\sigma}_{t_{i}}\!(x_{1}^{(i)})\!\cdot\!\epsilon_{i}\Big)}_{\text{Target }u_{t_{i}}}\right\|^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>General</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">+</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mtext>Input </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow></munder><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">−</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">+</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mtext>Target </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow></munder></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{General}}(\theta)\!=\!\frac{1}{B}\!\sum_{i=1}^{B}\left\|v_{\theta}\!\Big(\underbrace{\mu_{t_{i}}\!(x_{1}^{(i)})\!+\!\sigma_{t_{i}}\!(x_{1}^{(i)})\!\cdot\!\epsilon_{i}}_{\text{Input }x_{t_{i}}},\,t_{i}\Big)\!-\!\underbrace{\Big(\dot{\mu}_{t_{i}}\!(x_{1}^{(i)})\!+\!\dot{\sigma}_{t_{i}}\!(x_{1}^{(i)})\!\cdot\!\epsilon_{i}\Big)}_{\text{Target }u_{t_{i}}}\right\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix 0.D </span>Implementation Details of Scale-Adaptive Modulation</h2>

<div id="Pt0.A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide the detailed mathematical formulations and hyperparameter settings for the Scale-Adaptive Modulation strategies introduced in the main text. We outline the specific mechanisms applied to DiT-based and U-Net-based architectures.</p>
</div>
<section id="Pt0.A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">0.D.1 </span>Conditional Logit-Normal Sampling (For DiT Architectures)</h3>

<div id="Pt0.A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For MM-DiT backbones (specifically FLUX.1), the training process relies on a Logit-Normal distribution for time-step sampling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. To align the optimization dynamics with the frequency characteristics of our data slices, we introduce a Granularity-Dependent Shift to the sampling process.</p>
</div>
<section id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">The Sampling Chain.</h4>

<div id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> be a standard normal variable. The standard Logit-Normal sampling transforms <math id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> into a time step <math id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> via a sigmoid function. We modify the mean of this distribution based on the data granularity <math id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We define the Granularity Bias Function <math id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(S)</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A4.E29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A4.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(S)=\begin{cases}+0.5,&amp;\text{if }S=\text{Macro}\\
\phantom{+}0.0,&amp;\text{if }S=\text{Meso}\\
-0.5,&amp;\text{if }S=\text{Micro}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>Macro</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0.0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>Meso</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>Micro</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(S)=\begin{cases}+0.5,&amp;\text{if }S=\text{Macro}\\
\phantom{+}0.0,&amp;\text{if }S=\text{Meso}\\
-0.5,&amp;\text{if }S=\text{Micro}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The intermediate time variable <math id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{logit}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>logit</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{logit}}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed as:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A4.E30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A4.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{logit}}=\sigma(s\cdot z+\Delta(S))=\frac{1}{1+\exp(-(s\cdot z+\Delta(S)))}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>logit</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{logit}}=\sigma(s\cdot z+\Delta(S))=\frac{1}{1+\exp(-(s\cdot z+\Delta(S)))}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is the scale factor (set to <math id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0</annotation></semantics></math> in our experiments).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Resolution-Dependent Flow Shift.</h4>

<div id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following standard practice in FLUX, we further adjust the time step to account for image resolution, obtaining the final training time step <math id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> via a resolution-aware mapping:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A4.E31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A4.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=\frac{t_{\text{logit}}}{t_{\text{logit}}+(1-t_{\text{logit}})\cdot\exp(-\mu_{\text{res}})}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>logit</mtext></msub><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>logit</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>logit</mtext></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>res</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=\frac{t_{\text{logit}}}{t_{\text{logit}}+(1-t_{\text{logit}})\cdot\exp(-\mu_{\text{res}})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\text{res}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>res</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\text{res}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a linear function of the total pixel count <math id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\times W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\times W</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Effect on Optimization.</h4>

<div id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By setting <math id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\text{Macro})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>Macro</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\text{Macro})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the probability mass of <math id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> shifts towards <math id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> (high noise). This biases the model to prioritize low-frequency geometric reconstruction during the early phases of generation. Conversely, <math id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\text{Micro})&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>Micro</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\text{Micro})&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> shifts the mass towards <math id="Pt0.A4.SS1.SSSx3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> (low noise), encouraging high-frequency details to be refined under sustained gradient supervision in the late denoising stages.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">0.D.2 </span>Granularity-Adaptive Min-SNR Weighting (For U-Net Architectures)</h3>

<div id="Pt0.A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For U-Net architectures (<em id="Pt0.A4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="Pt0.A4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, SDXL), we adopt the Min-SNR weighting strategy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> to mitigate task conflict across noise levels. We extend this by introducing a Granularity-Adaptive Gamma Threshold.</p>
</div>
<section id="Pt0.A4.SS2.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR).</h4>

<div id="Pt0.A4.SS2.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A4.SS2.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define the SNR at time step <math id="Pt0.A4.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="Pt0.A4.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{SNR}(t)=\frac{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>SNR</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{SNR}(t)=\frac{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Pt0.A4.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\alpha}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\alpha}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> follows the standard noise schedule <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A4.SS2.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Granularity-Specific Clamping.</h4>

<div id="Pt0.A4.SS2.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A4.SS2.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The standard Min-SNR strategy clamps the loss weight at a fixed threshold <math id="Pt0.A4.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> (typically <math id="Pt0.A4.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=5.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=5.0</annotation></semantics></math>). We replace this global constant with a granularity-specific function <math id="Pt0.A4.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(S)</annotation></semantics></math>, formulated to balance structural and textural learning:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A4.E32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A4.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(S)=\begin{cases}7.0,&amp;\text{if }S=\text{Micro}\\
5.0,&amp;\text{if }S=\text{Meso}\\
4.0,&amp;\text{if }S=\text{Macro}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>7.0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>Micro</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>5.0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>Meso</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>4.0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>Macro</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(S)=\begin{cases}7.0,&amp;\text{if }S=\text{Micro}\\
5.0,&amp;\text{if }S=\text{Meso}\\
4.0,&amp;\text{if }S=\text{Macro}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A4.SS2.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">The Reweighting Function.</h4>

<div id="Pt0.A4.SS2.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A4.SS2.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the standard <math id="Pt0.A4.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-prediction objective, the final weighting term <math id="Pt0.A4.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(t,S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(t,S)</annotation></semantics></math> applied to the loss is defined as:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A4.E33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A4.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(t,S)=\frac{\min(\text{SNR}(t),\,\Gamma(S))}{\text{SNR}(t)}=\min\!\left(1,\,\frac{\Gamma(S)}{\text{SNR}(t)}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>SNR</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mtext>SNR</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.653em;" width="1.653em"><mi>min</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mtext>SNR</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(t,S)=\frac{\min(\text{SNR}(t),\,\Gamma(S))}{\text{SNR}(t)}=\min\!\left(1,\,\frac{\Gamma(S)}{\text{SNR}(t)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A4.SS2.SSSx4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Effect on Optimization.</h4>

<div id="Pt0.A4.SS2.SSSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A4.SS2.SSSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Pt0.A4.SS2.SSSx4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Micro-Boost</span> (<math id="Pt0.A4.SS2.SSSx4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma=7.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma=7.0</annotation></semantics></math>): By increasing the clamping threshold for Micro slices, the weighting function maintains a unit weight (<math id="Pt0.A4.SS2.SSSx4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0</annotation></semantics></math>) deeper into the high-SNR regime. This mitigates gradient decay during the late-stage phase of fine-detail reconstruction.</p>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A4.SS2.SSSx4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Pt0.A4.SS2.SSSx4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Pt0.A4.SS2.SSSx4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Macro-Relax</span> (<math id="Pt0.A4.SS2.SSSx4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma=4.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma=4.0</annotation></semantics></math>): By lowering the threshold for Macro slices, we accelerate weight decay as SNR increases. This regularizes the model, reducing its tendency to overfit to high-frequency noise when learning global structural concepts.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix 0.E </span>General Framework: Bridging Output-Space Residuals and Parameter-Space Gradient Dynamics</h2>

<div id="Pt0.A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The preceding sections establish that the optimization loss decomposes into a quadratic form governed by the Data Interference Matrix <math id="Pt0.A5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose entries <math id="Pt0.A5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{ij}=\Delta_{i}^{\top}\Delta_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{ij}=\Delta_{i}^{\top}\Delta_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> capture the geometric alignment of residual vectors in the <em id="Pt0.A5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">output space</em>. A natural and deeper question arises: does this output-space geometry faithfully reflect the actual optimization dynamics in the <em id="Pt0.A5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">parameter space</em>? In this section, we derive a general, architecture-agnostic framework that formally connects these two spaces through the Neural Tangent Kernel (NTK) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, thereby endowing the data-geometric analysis with concrete dynamical significance.</p>
</div>
<section id="Pt0.A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">0.E.1 </span>Recall of Core Definitions</h3>

<div id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by recalling the core quantities established in the preceding sections, which serve as the starting point for the gradient-space analysis.</p>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">From <a href="#Pt0.A1" title="Appendix 0.A Derivation of Granularity-Specific Vector Fields ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">0.A</span></a>, the true marginal vector field at any spacetime point <math id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> decomposes as a density-weighted superposition of granularity-specific sub-fields:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A5.E34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A5.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x)=\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)\,u_{t}^{\xi}(x),\quad\text{with}\quad\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)=1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>with</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x)=\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)\,u_{t}^{\xi}(x),\quad\text{with}\quad\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)=1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\xi}(x)=Z_{\xi}(x)/Z_{\text{total}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\xi}(x)=Z_{\xi}(x)/Z_{\text{total}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the <em id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">local data density</em> of source <math id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>—the probability that position <math id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is governed by sub-manifold <math id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">From <a href="#Pt0.A2" title="Appendix 0.B Proof of the Data Interference Matrix ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">0.B</span></a>, the Data Interference Matrix <math id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math> is constructed from the per-granularity <em id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">vector residuals</em>:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A5.E35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A5.E35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\xi}(x,t)=v_{\theta}(x,t)-u_{t}^{\xi}(x),\quad\xi\in\mathcal{T}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\xi}(x,t)=v_{\theta}(x,t)-u_{t}^{\xi}(x),\quad\xi\in\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(35)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">with entries <math id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{\xi\eta}=\langle\Delta_{\xi},\Delta_{\eta}\rangle=\Delta_{\xi}^{\top}\Delta_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\xi\eta}=\langle\Delta_{\xi},\Delta_{\eta}\rangle=\Delta_{\xi}^{\top}\Delta_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>. The local Flow Matching loss was shown to be the quadratic form (<a href="#Pt0.A2.E20" title="In 0.B.3 The Data Interference Matrix 𝛀 ‣ Appendix 0.B Proof of the Data Interference Matrix ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>):</p>
<table id="Pt0.A5.E36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A5.E36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\alpha}(x)^{\top}\mathbf{\Omega}(x)\,\boldsymbol{\alpha}(x)=\left\|\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)\,\Delta_{\xi}(x,t)\right\|^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝜶</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝜶</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\alpha}(x)^{\top}\mathbf{\Omega}(x)\,\boldsymbol{\alpha}(x)=\left\|\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)\,\Delta_{\xi}(x,t)\right\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(36)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Since for any scalar <math id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\arg\min_{\theta}f(\theta)=\arg\min_{\theta}c\,f(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\arg\min_{\theta}f(\theta)=\arg\min_{\theta}c\,f(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>, we adopt the rescaled form to streamline the subsequent gradient analysis. Hereafter, we redefine:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A5.E37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A5.E37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{Kernel}}(x)\triangleq\frac{1}{2}\left\|\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)\,\Delta_{\xi}(x,t)\right\|^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>Kernel</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≜</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{Kernel}}(x)\triangleq\frac{1}{2}\left\|\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)\,\Delta_{\xi}(x,t)\right\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(37)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This rescaling preserves the minimizer and all qualitative properties of the optimization landscape; the factor <math id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> serves solely to eliminate the constant <math id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> from the chain rule in the gradient derivations that follow.</p>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">These quantities—the density weights <math id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math>, the vector residuals <math id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math> composing <math id="Pt0.A5.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the quadratic loss structure—are the elements from which we now derive the gradient dynamics in parameter space.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">0.E.2 </span>Decomposition of the Global Residual via Partition of Unity</h3>

<div id="Pt0.A5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Partition of Unity property <math id="Pt0.A5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)=1</annotation></semantics></math> permits an identity expansion of the model output: <math id="Pt0.A5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\theta}(x,t)=\left(\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)\right)v_{\theta}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\theta}(x,t)=\left(\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)\right)v_{\theta}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math>. Combining this with the target decomposition (<a href="#Pt0.A5.E34" title="In 0.E.1 Recall of Core Definitions ‣ Appendix 0.E General Framework: Bridging Output-Space Residuals and Parameter-Space Gradient Dynamics ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a>), the global residual vector <math id="Pt0.A5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(x,t)=v_{\theta}(x,t)-u_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(x,t)=v_{\theta}(x,t)-u_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> decomposes exactly as:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A5.E38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A5.E38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(x,t)=\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)\big(v_{\theta}(x,t)-u_{t}^{\xi}(x)\big)=\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)\,\Delta_{\xi}(x,t)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(x,t)=\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)\big(v_{\theta}(x,t)-u_{t}^{\xi}(x)\big)=\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)\,\Delta_{\xi}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(38)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This identity is the algebraic foundation for connecting the output-space geometry to parameter-space dynamics: the global residual is a density-weighted superposition of per-granularity residuals.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">0.E.3 </span>From the Global Residual to Parameter Gradients</h3>

<div id="Pt0.A5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Pt0.A5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{\theta}(x,t)\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{\theta}(x,t)\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times P}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the Jacobian matrix of the network output with respect to its parameters:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A5.E39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A5.E39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{\theta}(x,t)=\frac{\partial v_{\theta}(x,t)}{\partial\theta}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{\theta}(x,t)=\frac{\partial v_{\theta}(x,t)}{\partial\theta}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(39)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Applying the chain rule to the global loss (<a href="#Pt0.A5.E37" title="In 0.E.1 Recall of Core Definitions ‣ Appendix 0.E General Framework: Bridging Output-Space Residuals and Parameter-Space Gradient Dynamics ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">37</span></a>), the total parameter gradient is:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A5.E40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A5.E40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\text{total}}=\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}_{\text{Kernel}}(x)=J_{\theta}(x,t)^{\top}e(x,t)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>Kernel</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\text{total}}=\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}_{\text{Kernel}}(x)=J_{\theta}(x,t)^{\top}e(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(40)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Substituting the residual decomposition (<a href="#Pt0.A5.E38" title="In 0.E.2 Decomposition of the Global Residual via Partition of Unity ‣ Appendix 0.E General Framework: Bridging Output-Space Residuals and Parameter-Space Gradient Dynamics ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">38</span></a>) and invoking linearity of the matrix-vector product:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A5.E41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A5.E41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\text{total}}=J_{\theta}(x,t)^{\top}\left(\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)\,\Delta_{\xi}(x,t)\right)=\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)\,J_{\theta}(x,t)^{\top}\Delta_{\xi}(x,t)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\text{total}}=J_{\theta}(x,t)^{\top}\left(\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)\,\Delta_{\xi}(x,t)\right)=\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\alpha_{\xi}(x)\,J_{\theta}(x,t)^{\top}\Delta_{\xi}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(41)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This reveals the key structural insight: each output-space residual <math id="Pt0.A5.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\xi}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\xi}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="Pt0.A5.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">lifted</em> into the parameter space <math id="Pt0.A5.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>P</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{P}</annotation></semantics></math> via the transposed Jacobian <math id="Pt0.A5.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{\theta}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{\theta}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, which acts as a linear map from the output tangent space to the parameter tangent space. We therefore <em id="Pt0.A5.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">define</em> the gradient component attributable to granularity <math id="Pt0.A5.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A5.E42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A5.E42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\xi}\triangleq\alpha_{\xi}(x)\,J_{\theta}(x,t)^{\top}\Delta_{\xi}(x,t)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo>≜</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\xi}\triangleq\alpha_{\xi}(x)\,J_{\theta}(x,t)^{\top}\Delta_{\xi}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(42)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A5.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Pt0.A5.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark.</span> Crucially, <math id="Pt0.A5.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="Pt0.A5.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> defined as the gradient of an isolated per-granularity loss <math id="Pt0.A5.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}\alpha_{\xi}\|\Delta_{\xi}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}\alpha_{\xi}\|\Delta_{\xi}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is the mathematically exact component of the total gradient <math id="Pt0.A5.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\text{total}}=\sum_{\xi}g_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></msub><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\text{total}}=\sum_{\xi}g_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math> that arises from the density-weighted decomposition of the global residual, preserving the physical meaning of <math id="Pt0.A5.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math> as the local field density throughout.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">0.E.4 </span>Gradient Dynamics and the Emergence of the NTK</h3>

<div id="Pt0.A5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The optimization dynamics are governed by the squared norm of the total gradient, which decomposes via the bilinear structure of the inner product:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A5.E43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A5.E43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{\text{total}}\|^{2}=\left\|\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}g_{\xi}\right\|^{2}=\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{T}}\langle g_{\xi},g_{\eta}\rangle" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{\text{total}}\|^{2}=\left\|\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}g_{\xi}\right\|^{2}=\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{T}}\sum_{\eta\in\mathcal{T}}\langle g_{\xi},g_{\eta}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(43)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Expanding any pairwise gradient inner product using <a href="#Pt0.A5.E42" title="In 0.E.3 From the Global Residual to Parameter Gradients ‣ Appendix 0.E General Framework: Bridging Output-Space Residuals and Parameter-Space Gradient Dynamics ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">42</span></a>:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A5.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="Pt0.A5.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Pt0.A5.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\langle g_{\xi},g_{\eta}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\langle g_{\xi},g_{\eta}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Pt0.A5.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\left(\alpha_{\xi}J_{\theta}^{\top}\Delta_{\xi}\right)^{\top}\left(\alpha_{\eta}J_{\theta}^{\top}\Delta_{\eta}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left(\alpha_{\xi}J_{\theta}^{\top}\Delta_{\xi}\right)^{\top}\left(\alpha_{\eta}J_{\theta}^{\top}\Delta_{\eta}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="Pt0.A5.E44"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Pt0.A5.E44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\alpha_{\xi}\alpha_{\eta}\,\Delta_{\xi}^{\top}\underbrace{\left(J_{\theta}\,J_{\theta}^{\top}\right)}_{\Theta_{\theta}(x,t)}\Delta_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\alpha_{\xi}\alpha_{\eta}\,\Delta_{\xi}^{\top}\underbrace{\left(J_{\theta}\,J_{\theta}^{\top}\right)}_{\Theta_{\theta}(x,t)}\Delta_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(44)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A5.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The matrix <math id="Pt0.A5.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{\theta}(x,t)=J_{\theta}(x,t)J_{\theta}(x,t)^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times d_{\text{out}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{\theta}(x,t)=J_{\theta}(x,t)J_{\theta}(x,t)^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times d_{\text{out}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is precisely the local Neural Tangent Kernel (NTK) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. This yields the central result of this section:</p>
<table id="Pt0.A5.E45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Pt0.A5.E45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boxed{\;\langle g_{\xi},g_{\eta}\rangle=\alpha_{\xi}\alpha_{\eta}\,\Delta_{\xi}^{\top}\,\Theta_{\theta}(x,t)\,\Delta_{\eta}\;}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boxed{\;\langle g_{\xi},g_{\eta}\rangle=\alpha_{\xi}\alpha_{\eta}\,\Delta_{\xi}^{\top}\,\Theta_{\theta}(x,t)\,\Delta_{\eta}\;}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(45)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">0.E.5 </span>On the Intractability of the NTK and the Case for Output-Space Intervention</h3>

<div id="Pt0.A5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#Pt0.A5.E45" title="In 0.E.4 Gradient Dynamics and the Emergence of the NTK ‣ Appendix 0.E General Framework: Bridging Output-Space Residuals and Parameter-Space Gradient Dynamics ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">45</span></a> reveals that the true gradient interference is governed by <math id="Pt0.A5.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\xi}^{\top}\Theta_{\theta}\Delta_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\xi}^{\top}\Theta_{\theta}\Delta_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>, not the bare residual inner product <math id="Pt0.A5.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\xi}^{\top}\Delta_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\xi}^{\top}\Delta_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>. In principle, the NTK matrix <math id="Pt0.A5.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> can amplify, attenuate, or rotate the interference pattern relative to <math id="Pt0.A5.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math>. We do <em id="Pt0.A5.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> assume that <math id="Pt0.A5.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is isotropic—for modern generative architectures with billions of parameters, heterogeneous layer types, and attention mechanisms, such an assumption would be unrealistic.</p>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A5.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, direct intervention on <math id="Pt0.A5.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is equally intractable: the NTK is an implicit function of the entire network architecture, the current parameter state, and the input <math id="Pt0.A5.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math>. It cannot be analytically computed, stably estimated, or meaningfully controlled during training for models at the scale of FLUX or SDXL.</p>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A5.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We therefore adopt a pragmatic strategy grounded in the bilinear structure of <a href="#Pt0.A5.E45" title="In 0.E.4 Gradient Dynamics and the Emergence of the NTK ‣ Appendix 0.E General Framework: Bridging Output-Space Residuals and Parameter-Space Gradient Dynamics ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">45</span></a>: since <math id="Pt0.A5.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle g_{\xi},g_{\eta}\rangle=\alpha_{\xi}\alpha_{\eta}\,\Delta_{\xi}^{\top}\Theta_{\theta}\,\Delta_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle g_{\xi},g_{\eta}\rangle=\alpha_{\xi}\alpha_{\eta}\,\Delta_{\xi}^{\top}\Theta_{\theta}\,\Delta_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>, the gradient inner product is <em id="Pt0.A5.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">jointly</em> determined by the residual vectors <math id="Pt0.A5.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\xi},\Delta_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\xi},\Delta_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> and the kernel <math id="Pt0.A5.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>. While we cannot control <math id="Pt0.A5.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, we <em id="Pt0.A5.SS5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">can</em> control the statistical structure of the residuals through data curation and sampling design:</p>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A5.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="Pt0.A5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="Pt0.A5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="Pt0.A5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Pt0.A5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hierarchical Dataset Construction</span>: By organizing training data into structurally coherent granularities (Macro, Meso, Micro), we structure the residual vectors <math id="Pt0.A5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math> such that they encode well-separated frequency content, aiming to reduce the magnitude of destructive cross-scale inner products <math id="Pt0.A5.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\xi}^{\top}\Theta_{\theta}\Delta_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\xi}^{\top}\Theta_{\theta}\Delta_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> without requiring specific knowledge of the spectral structure of <math id="Pt0.A5.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Pt0.A5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="Pt0.A5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Pt0.A5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tuple-wise Co-sampling</span>: Mathematically, since the NTK is defined as <math id="Pt0.A5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{\theta}=J_{\theta}J_{\theta}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{\theta}=J_{\theta}J_{\theta}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, it is inherently a positive semi-definite (PSD) matrix. However, due to its high dimensionality and structural complexity, a PSD matrix does not possess sign preservation for inner products. That is, even if the output-space residuals are aligned (<math id="Pt0.A5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\xi}^{\top}\Delta_{\eta}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\xi}^{\top}\Delta_{\eta}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>), the actual parameter-space gradient interference <math id="Pt0.A5.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\xi}^{\top}\Theta_{\theta}\Delta_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\xi}^{\top}\Theta_{\theta}\Delta_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> may still be negative (destructive interference) due to the rotational effects of the NTK. Drawing from widespread empirical observations in Multi-Task Learning (MTL) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, if gradient updates are consecutively dominated by a single task (or granularity), the optimization trajectory suffers from severe oscillation and drift. To mitigate this, we intervene directly in the output residual space: by co-sampling heterogeneous granularities within the same optimization step, we promote synchronous updates. This paradigm encourages the optimizer to navigate the superposition of gradients, aiming to reduce both the amplitude and frequency of oscillations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Pt0.A5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="Pt0.A5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Pt0.A5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scale-Adaptive Modulation</span>: Mathematically, the convergence rate of gradient descent is governed by the condition number of the interaction matrix <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, where diagonal dominance ensures stable and accelerated optimization. Furthermore, the NTK exhibits a well-documented Spectral Bias <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, inherently learning low-frequency components faster than high-frequency ones. By conditioning the time-step distribution on granularity, we explicitly align the data frequency with the corresponding eigenspectrum of the NTK. Operating each sub-manifold in a well-suited noise regime is expected to amplify its self-alignment signal (<math id="Pt0.A5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\xi}^{\top}\Theta_{\theta}\Delta_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\xi}^{\top}\Theta_{\theta}\Delta_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math>), encouraging the local gradient interaction matrix toward greater diagonal dominance. This is intended to act as a heuristic preconditioning mechanism, promoting the constructive component of the NTK-projected gradient while mitigating spectral conflation.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A5.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, the SGA framework does not require the NTK to be well-conditioned or isotropic. Instead, it promotes optimization stability by shaping the <em id="Pt0.A5.SS5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">input</em> to the bilinear form—the residual vectors <math id="Pt0.A5.SS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math>—through principled data and sampling design, so that the effective gradient <math id="Pt0.A5.SS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\text{total}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\text{total}}</annotation></semantics></math> is more likely to remain within a cone of stable descent even under an unknown and complex <math id="Pt0.A5.SS5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A5.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">0.E.6 </span>Broader Implications: A Speculative Connection to Scaling Laws</h3>

<div id="Pt0.A5.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following discussion is speculative but suggestive. While the primary focus of this work is to resolve cross-scale gradient conflicts during generative fine-tuning, the quadratic optimization geometry and NTK framework derived in <a href="#Pt0.A5" title="Appendix 0.E General Framework: Bridging Output-Space Residuals and Parameter-Space Gradient Dynamics ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">0.E</span></a> provide a lens through which to interpret the empirical successes of Scaling Laws <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>. We hypothesize that the macroscopic benefits of scaling data (<math id="Pt0.A5.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>) and batch size (<math id="Pt0.A5.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>) may be partly tied to the stabilization of the Data Interference Matrix <math id="Pt0.A5.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math> and the well-conditioning of the local vector field.</p>
</div>
<section id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Large Batch Sizes Enable Accurate Estimation of Population Interference.</h4>

<div id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As established in <a href="#Pt0.A5.E45" title="In 0.E.4 Gradient Dynamics and the Emergence of the NTK ‣ Appendix 0.E General Framework: Bridging Output-Space Residuals and Parameter-Space Gradient Dynamics ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">45</span></a>, the true gradient interference between any two data samples is governed by their NTK-projected residuals: <math id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle g_{\xi},g_{\eta}\rangle=\alpha_{\xi}\alpha_{\eta}\Delta_{\xi}^{\top}\Theta_{\theta}(x,t)\Delta_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle g_{\xi},g_{\eta}\rangle=\alpha_{\xi}\alpha_{\eta}\Delta_{\xi}^{\top}\Theta_{\theta}(x,t)\Delta_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>. In standard training with a small batch size <math id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, the empirical estimation of these gradient interactions suffers from high statistical variance. The optimization trajectory can be significantly influenced by stochastic, local sampling noise rather than the true geometric structure of the data manifold. As the dataset scales, permitting a proportionally larger <math id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, the empirical batch-level gradient interactions reliably approximate the true population expected interference. This allows the optimizer to descend along a trajectory that more closely reflects the global NTK geometry.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Heterogeneous Co-occurrence Explicitly Resolves Cross-Term Conflicts.</h4>

<div id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond variance reduction, scaling <math id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> fundamentally alters the interaction dynamics within the Data Interference Matrix <math id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math>. With a small <math id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, a single optimization step is highly likely to sample homogeneous data. Consequently, the off-diagonal cross-interference terms in <math id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math> are frequently absent from the current update step, leading to isolated feature fitting and subsequent gradient conflicts across iterations. Conversely, a massive <math id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> greatly increases the likelihood of co-occurrence of a broadly heterogeneous set of samples. This macroscopic diversity forces the optimizer to simultaneously navigate the complete, fully-populated geometry of <math id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math>. By explicitly confronting and resolving these heterogeneous cross-term conflicts at every step, the model is encouraged to converge toward a geometric equilibrium that tends to generalize better.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Massive Datasets Well-Condition the Target Vector Field Density.</h4>

<div id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From a purely data-centric perspective, scaling the total sample size <math id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> directly improves the mathematical properties of the learning objective. As shown in <a href="#Pt0.A1.E12" title="In 0.A.2 The Marginal Vector Field as a Nadaraya-Watson Estimator ‣ Appendix 0.A Derivation of Granularity-Specific Vector Fields ‣ The Quadratic Geometry of Flow Matching: Semantic Granularity Alignment for Text-to-Image Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, the optimal marginal vector field <math id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> functions as a Nadaraya-Watson kernel regression estimator. When <math id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is small, the empirical distribution is sparse. Therefore, at any inference point <math id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, the local density weights <math id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\alpha}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\alpha}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> are overly influenced by a few outlier data points, resulting in a rugged, highly non-linear target vector field. As <math id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> scales exponentially, the empirical manifold densely and uniformly populates the true data manifold. This macroscopic scaling tends to “well-condition” the microscopic local density <math id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\alpha}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\alpha}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, promoting a smoother probability assignment. From the perspective of our quadratic form <math id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{local}}(x)=\boldsymbol{\alpha}^{\top}\mathbf{\Omega}\boldsymbol{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>local</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝜶</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{local}}(x)=\boldsymbol{\alpha}^{\top}\mathbf{\Omega}\boldsymbol{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, a smooth and well-conditioned density field tends to reduce the Lipschitz constant of the target vector field, making the residual field <math id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> inherently easier to fit.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Summary: SGA as a Structured Analogy to Scaling Laws.</h4>

<div id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In conclusion, under compute- and data-constrained fine-tuning scenarios, the proposed Semantic Granularity Alignment (SGA) framework can be viewed as a structured analogy to certain optimization benefits typically associated with Scaling Laws.</p>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, the H-SD mechanism explicitly expands the number of valid training samples <math id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> utilized for generative modeling. This physical scale expansion is expected to help smooth the estimation of the local target vector field density. Simultaneously, by hierarchically decomposing the data manifold, H-SD disentangles complex, interwoven signals, allowing crucial semantic features to be presented independently across distinct slices, thereby lowering the fitting difficulty of the gradient update field.</p>
</div>
<div id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, the other two core components of SGA—Tuple-wise Optimization and Scale-Adaptive Modulation—are designed to execute targeted geometric interventions under the objective constraint of small-batch updates. This structured co-sampling and frequency band modulation help suppress the gradient oscillations induced by heterogeneous cross-terms and reduce the statistical variance of single-step updates. In doing so, SGA strives to closely approximate the accurate, global population interference estimation that is otherwise typically achieved only with a massive batch size <math id="Pt0.A5.SS6.SSSx4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. Ultimately, SGA represents an attempt to partially compensate for macroscopic scale disadvantages through microscopic prior design, and aims to achieve this by aligning the intrinsic geometry of the data with the underlying optimization dynamics.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix 0.F </span>Full Experimental Setup</h2>

